      "answer": null,
      "reason": "Long sight is corrected with a converging lens, and both A (biconvex lens) and B (convex lens) are converging lenses, so both are correct on the biology and the physics. The case for B is that convex lens is the usual textbook wording and the more general term, and real spectacle lenses are usually meniscus rather than biconvex. That distinction is beyond the syllabus, and nothing in the stem lets a student tell whether the setter meant biconvex or convex as the key; the options were built as two converging and two diverging lenses, so the setter saw a difference that secondary teaching does not. Keying B would teach students that a biconvex lens does not correct long sight, which is false, so no single key should be forced."
